## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to add observability with OpenTelemetry in a docker-dca context, ensuring end-to-end trace propagation from edge to service and basic validation.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry instrumentation and OTLP exporter
- otel-collector as a sidecar and Jaeger backend
- Trace context propagation (traceparent/tracestate)
- docker-compose scaffolding for api, nginx, collector
- Basic end-to-end validation via Jaeger UI

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js OpenTelemetry setup (simplified)
const { NodeTracerProvider } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node');
const { OTLPTraceExporter } = require('@opentelemetry/exporter-otlp-http');
const { SimpleSpanProcessor } = require('@opentelemetry/tracing');

const provider = new NodeTracerProvider();
provider.addSpanProcessor(new SimpleSpanProcessor(
  new OTLPTraceExporter({ url: 'http://otel-collector:4318/v1/traces' })
));
provider.register();
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust sampling and metric correlation to avoid perf impact on a busy API?
- How would you test trace failures or collector downtime without losing observability?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement data governance and privacy in a multi-DC setup: edge TLS, per-tenant policy, hot-reload, and observability with Loki, all integrated via docker-dca.

## Key Concepts

- Traefik edge TLS termination across DCs
- Middleware-based response redaction per-tenant
- Docker Config hot-reload and policy delivery
- Loki-based centralized logs with per-tenant retention
- X-Tenant routing and impact on latency

## Code Example

```javascript
// Express-style middleware skeleton for redacting response payloads per-tenant policy
function redactor(policy) {
  return (req, res, next) => {
    const oldSend = res.send;
    res.send = function (body) {
      let payload = typeof body === 'string' ? JSON.parse(body) : body;
      const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'];
      const p = policy[tenant] || { redact: [] };
      if (payload && typeof payload === 'object') {
        for (const key of p.redact) {
          if (payload.hasOwnProperty(key)) payload[key] = 'REDACTED';
        }
      }
      oldSend.call(this, JSON.stringify(payload));
    };
    next();
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test hot-reload correctness and no-downtime guarantees for policy updates?
- How would you measure and mitigate the performance impact of redaction on large payloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical ability to extend a docker-dca setup with a compliant, low-latency data-redaction path.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config-driven per-tenant policies
- Sidecar pattern and in-memory caching
- Streaming JSON redaction without full buffering
- Nginx-edge integration and TLS context

## Code Example
```go
package main
import (
  "encoding/json"
  "fmt"
)
func redact(m map[string]interface{}, fields map[string]bool){
  for k,v := range m {
    if fields[k] { m[k] = "***REDACTED***"; continue }
    if mm, ok := v.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(mm, fields) }
    if arr, ok := v.([]interface{}); ok {
      for _, it := range arr { if sub, ok := it.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(sub, fields) } }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test nested JSON redaction with large bodies?
- How would you monitor redaction performance without affecting latency?
